    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A deck is more than an extension of the house; it is an outdoor room, a place for gatherings and a real investment in how you use your garden. Yet the part that matters most for safety and looks, the railing, is usually the most exposed to the weather. Whether your railing is pressure-treated timber, sleek aluminium, glass or composite, it needs a regular maintenance routine to stay safe and attractive.</p>
<p>Neglected railings can loosen, stain and eventually need replacing far sooner than they should. Working through the year season by season lets you deal with each kind of weather as it arrives. This guide sets out the key jobs for every season and how they change with the railing material. Always read your railing or decking manufacturer's care guide as well, as products differ.</p>

<h2 id="spring">1. Spring: inspect and clean</h2>
<p>Spring is the most important time for railing care. After months of rain, frost and perhaps snow, your railings need a thorough check and a good clean before the busy summer months.</p>
<p>Start with an inspection. Look along the whole run of the railing for posts that have shifted or leaned, which can happen as the ground freezes and thaws. Pay most attention to where the posts meet the deck frame: these connections carry the load when someone leans on the rail, so if they are loose the whole railing is weakened. Tighten any bolts or screws that have worked loose, and note anything that will not tighten.</p>
<p>Next, tackle the winter grime. Mould, mildew and algae thrive in cool, damp conditions.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Timber:</strong> use a cleaner made for exterior wood that deals with organic growth without stripping the timber.</li>
<li><strong>Aluminium, vinyl and composite:</strong> warm water with a little mild washing-up liquid is usually enough.</li>
<li><strong>All materials:</strong> work dirt out of corners and joints with a soft-bristle brush, then rinse with a garden hose and spray nozzle.</li>
</ul>
<p>Avoid blasting timber with a pressure washer, as high pressure can raise the grain and leave it fuzzy or splintered (see the FAQ below).</p>
<p>Finally, check timber railings for rot. Press a screwdriver gently into the base of each post and any joints that stay damp. If the wood feels soft or spongy, the post may need repair or replacement. Catching rot in spring gives you time to deal with it before it spreads.</p>

<h2 id="summer">2. Summer: protect from the sun</h2>
<p>Where spring is about recovery, summer is about protection. The main enemy now is sunlight. UV breaks down the surface of timber, turning it grey and causing fine cracks, and it can make some lower-quality vinyl or composite fade or become brittle over time.</p>
<p>Midsummer is a good moment to check the finish on timber railings with a simple water test. Sprinkle a few drops of water on the handrail. If they bead up, the finish is still protecting the wood; if they soak in straight away, the sealer or stain has worn through and the timber is open to sun and summer downpours. Make a note to refinish in early autumn.</p>
<p>Glass railings need cleaning mainly for looks and visibility: a streak-free glass cleaner removes fingerprints, pollen and dust. On metal railings, watch for a chalky or peeling finish, and remember that dark metal handrails can get very hot in direct sun. Touch up or recoat the finish using a product the railing manufacturer recommends.</p>
<p>Summer also brings the most guests leaning on and gripping the rails, so check stability every few weeks. Take hold of each post and push and pull it firmly. It should feel solid. If anything moves, look under the deck to see whether the bolts fixing the posts to the frame have loosened, and see the safety FAQ below.</p>

<h2 id="autumn">3. Autumn: clear debris and refinish</h2>
<p>As temperatures drop and leaves fall, the focus shifts to clearing debris and keeping moisture away. Damp that sits against railings through autumn is what leads to rot in timber and corrosion in metal.</p>
<p>Leaves, pine needles and seeds collect in the corners where posts meet the deck boards. Once wet, they form a soggy mat that holds water against the base of the posts, speeding up rot in timber and staining or marking metal and composite. Clear these spots with a leaf blower or stiff broom, ideally weekly while the leaves are falling.</p>
<p>Early autumn is also usually your last chance for "wet work" before winter. If your summer water test showed that timber railings need a fresh coat of stain or sealer, choose a dry spell and apply it within the temperature and dry-weather window given on the product label. A fresh finish gives the wood a barrier against the wet months ahead.</p>
<p>On metal railings, look for scratches or chips in the powder coating. Even small breaks can let moisture reach the bare metal and start rust. Seal them with the manufacturer's recommended touch-up kit before winter sets in.</p>

<h2 id="winter">4. Winter: snow, ice and storm checks</h2>
<p>Winter care is less about cleaning and more about avoiding damage and keeping the deck safe to use.</p>
<p>Keep metal shovels away from the railings. A metal blade can gouge timber, scratch powder-coated aluminium or chip glass. Use a plastic shovel without a metal edge, and take care not to knock the posts as you clear the deck.</p>
<p>Be careful with de-icers. Avoid rock salt anywhere near the railing: it can corrode metal fixings and dry out timber. If you need a de-icer for safety, use one that your decking and railing manufacturers approve, use it sparingly, and rinse the railing and boards thoroughly once the cold weather is over.</p>
<p>After heavy snow, brush away deep drifts that build up against the railing rather than leaving them to sit, as a large mass of snow pushing against the infill or balusters puts the railing under strain. After any big storm, check that no branches have fallen on the railing and that every section still feels firm.</p>

<h2 id="materials">5. Care by railing material</h2>
<p>The seasonal routine applies to every deck, but the details depend on what your railing is made of.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Timber railings:</strong> the most maintenance. Clean them every year and refinish every few years, or as the product label advises; railings in sunnier, more exposed spots will need it sooner. Watch the end grain on the tops of posts, which soaks up water most readily. Post caps shield the end grain from rain and help the posts last longer.</li>
<li><strong>Aluminium railings:</strong> very durable, but the finish can oxidise and turn chalky, especially near the coast. A seasonal wipe-down and a protectant the manufacturer recommends keeps the finish looking fresh.</li>
<li><strong>Composite railings:</strong> they do not rot like timber, but they can grow mould and pick up tannin stains from leaves left lying on them. Wash them each season with a cleaner the manufacturer approves.</li>
<li><strong>Glass railings:</strong> the hardware is the part to watch. The clamps, clips or channels holding the panels can loosen, and the rubber gaskets that cushion the glass can perish or slip. Check them each season against the manufacturer's guidance. If a panel is chipped, cracked or damaged, stop using that section and have the glass replaced professionally.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Walk the railing with a notebook at the start of each season and number the posts. Writing down "post 4 slightly loose" or "post 7 soft at base" makes it easy to see whether a problem is getting worse, and gives a builder a clear list if you need help.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>How often should I restain wooden deck railings?</h3>
<p>Every few years, or as the stain or sealer label advises. Sun exposure makes the biggest difference: railings in a sunny, exposed spot will need refinishing sooner than shaded ones. The summer water test is the best guide; once water soaks in rather than beading, it is time.</p>
<h3>Can I use a pressure washer on my railings?</h3>
<p>Only with care, and only if the manufacturer allows it. Use a low setting and a fan nozzle, held well back, following the manufacturer's guidance, and keep the wand moving. Too much pressure can etch timber and lift finishes. Avoid pressure washing glass panels and powder-coated metal if the maker advises against it; a brush and hose are safer.</p>
<h3>How do I deal with rust on railing fixings?</h3>
<p>For light surface rust on bolts or screws, remove it with a wire brush and apply a rust-inhibiting treatment. If a fixing is badly corroded, replace it with a stainless steel or galvanised one suitable for your railing and timber, or ask a builder to do so if it is a structural bolt.</p>
<h3>Why is my composite railing fading?</h3>
<p>Older composite products were more prone to UV fading; modern capped composites resist it much better. If yours is fading, contact the manufacturer for advice, as some offer restorative products. Composite is low maintenance, not no maintenance.</p>
<h3>How do I know if my railing is still safe?</h3>
<p>Check two things: the gaps and the stability. Rules on guarding vary, but UK Building Regulations and many US codes use a 100 mm (4 in) sphere test for domestic guarding, meaning a sphere of that size should not pass through any opening; check the rules where you live. For stability, push firmly on each post and section of rail. If any post wobbles or the rail moves, keep children off the deck and have it checked by a qualified builder before using it again.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A short check and clean each season keeps your railings safe to lean on and stops small problems, such as a loose bolt or a patch of soft timber, from turning into costly repairs. For care of the boards themselves, read our <a href="/blog/composite-deck-maintenance-by-season">composite deck maintenance by season</a> guide, or if you are still choosing materials, see <a href="/blog/wood-deck-pros-and-cons">wood deck pros and cons</a> and the <a href="/blog/composite-deck-mistakes">composite deck mistakes</a> to avoid. Explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service for styling your finished deck, and to plan your outdoor space with a designer, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
